<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Preparing for Exam 1</title>

  <p component="prod-04">
    Our first exam is <alert>Wednesday, October 7</alert> during
    our usual class meeting time.  We will take some time on Monday,
    October 5 to review a bit, but it would be a good idea to start
    earlier.
  </p>

  <p component="prod-02">
    Our first exam is <alert>Thursday, October 8</alert> during
    our usual class meeting time.  We will take some time on Tuesday,
    October 6 to review a bit, but it would be a good idea to start
    earlier.
  </p>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Topics</title>

    <p>
      The exam will cover everything that we've done in the course up to
      this point.  One of the best things to do is to work through the
      in-class activities and make sure that you feel comfortable with
      everything.  Solutions to all of those activities are available on
      Blackboard, but you can also print new copies using the printer
      icon and work through them again.
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Remember:</alert> The most important thing is for you to
      focus on concepts.  It's also great to find an answer, but be
      sure that you understand all the steps that you've taken to get
      there and how they fit together.  If you have this kind of
      understanding, you should be able to deal with situations that
      may be slightly new.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here is a list of topics that we have studied.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            This is something from Calculus I, but it's important to
            remember how to evaluate a definite integral.  In
            particular,
            <md>
              \int_a^b f(x)~dx = F(b)-F(a)
            </md>
            if <m>F</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Antiderivatives:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  We have seen how to use an integral to define an
                  antiderivative of a function.  We called this the
                  <em>Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.</em>
                  That is, if
                  <md>
                    F(x)=\int_a^x f(t)~dt
                  </md>,
                  then <m>F' = f</m>, which means that <m>F</m> is an
                  antiderivative of <m>f</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  We have also seen three techniques for symbolically
                  finding antiderivatives:
                  <ol>
                    <li>Table of antiderivatives</li>
                    <li>Substitution</li>
                    <li>Integration by parts</li>
                  </ol>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            We have also seen some ways of approximating definite
            integrals:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Left and right Riemann sums
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The midpoint rule
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The trapezoidal rule
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Slice-compute-add: This is our main principle for looking
            at applications.  Integrals show up in many, many places
            and in many, many disciplines so it's important to be
            familiar with how to set up integrals using this
            principle.  We have applied this to the following
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Area
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Volume
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Mass
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Centers of mass
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Work
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Time</title>

    <p>
      Just a reminder:  I realize that in-class exams can be stressful
      because of the time constraints.  I take this seriously and will
      design the exam so that you can easily complete it in our class
      meeting (one hour, fifty minutes).  Your job is to make sure you
      are prepared.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

</worksheet>
